{"name":"Camouflage Olive","hex":"#a28f5c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camouflage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A disguise or covering up.","The act of disguising."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hide or disguise something by covering it up or changing its appearance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camouflage"},{"term":"olive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of species Olea europaea cultivated since ancient times in the Mediterranean for its fruit and the oil obtained from it.","The small oval fruit of this tree, eaten ripe (usually black) or unripe (usually green)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish green color, that of an unripe olive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camouflage","blocks":[{"text":"Camouflage is the use of any combination of materials, coloration, or illumination for concealment, either by making animals or objects hard to see, or by disguising them as something else. Examples include the leopard's spotted coat, the battledress of a modern soldier, and the leaf-mimic katydid's wings. A third approach, motion dazzle, confuses the observer with a conspicuous pattern, making the object visible but momentarily harder to locate. The majority of camouflage methods aim for crypsis, often through a general resemblance to the background, high contrast disruptive coloration, eliminating shadow, and countershading. In the open ocean, where there is no background, the principal methods of camouflage are transparency, silvering, and countershading, while the ability to produce light is among other things used for counter-illumination on the undersides of cephalopods such as squid. Some animals, such as chameleons and octopuses, are capable of actively changing their skin pattern and colours, whether for camouflage or for signalling. It is possible that some plants use camouflage to evade being eaten by herbivores."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Flower_flounder_in_Kona_may_2010.jpg/330px-Flower_flounder_in_Kona_may_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camouflage"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Maskowanie.JPEG/500px-Maskowanie.JPEG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Maskowanie.JPEG/500px-Maskowanie.JPEG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A soldier applying camouflage face paint; both helmet and jacket are disruptively patterned.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaskowanie.JPEG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg/500px-Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg/1280px-Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg/500px-Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Octopuses like this Octopus cyanea can change colour (and shape) for camouflage","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOctopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg/500px-Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg/500px-Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Experiment by Poulton, 1890: swallowtailed moth pupae with camouflage they acquired as larvae","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVariable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/PeacockInTheWoods.jpg/500px-PeacockInTheWoods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/PeacockInTheWoods.jpg/500px-PeacockInTheWoods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Abbott Thayer's 1907 painting Peacock in the Woods depicted a peacock as if it were camouflaged.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeacockInTheWoods.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.o